  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Generally, the greater the valence of the flocculating ion added, the greater its power to cause precipitation. In the coagulation of a negative sol, the flocculating power is in the order:&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Al</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Ba</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math>&nbsp;forms a negative sol.</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AlCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;coagulate it most.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
